    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`. It builds, and `go vet` and `go test` pass, but the repo has no test files and no database was available, so none of the database behaviour has actually been run. That includes row-level security, the advisory lock, concurrency, the 503 rollback on a failed write, and replays.\n\nIt's in a new file, `internal/candidate/reopen.go`. In `approval.go` I wrapped the existing bulk handler: `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", newReopen(pool), …)` now sits in front of it. Any request that isn't a reopen falls through unchanged, so bulk-approve, bulk-reject and the original API keep their behaviour. As in bulk-reject, everything happens in one transaction:\n\n- **Concurrency:** it sets the tenant, user and role, then locks on (`reopen.v5`, tenant, user, key). Two requests with the same key run one after the other, so the second one replays.\n- **Replays:** if an `ActionRequest` row already exists, a different purchase ID gets 409. The same one gets the stored response back with `Idempotency-Replayed: true` and no new writes. The response bytes are identical because both encode the same struct.\n- **New requests:** a single update moves the purchase from `rejected` to `submitted`, limited to the current tenant and the caller as creator. A missing, foreign or non-rejected purchase gets 409, so it never reveals whether a purchase exists. If two different keys hit the same purchase, the second waits on the row lock, finds it already `submitted`, and gets 409.\n- **Rows written:** an `ApprovalAudit` row (`rejected`→`submitted`, with the reason), a `purchase.reopened` event in `PurchaseEvent`, and the `ActionRequest` row. If any insert fails, the whole transaction rolls back and the client gets 503, so the same key can be retried.\n\nWhy the extra rows and the hash format:\n- **`ApprovalAudit` row:** `ledger.Drift` checks each purchase's status against its latest audit row. Without this row it would flag every reopened purchase.\n- **Event:** `notify` already has a recipient group set up for `purchase.reopened` events.\n- **`requestHash`:** it is `Fingerprint(\"reopen.v5\", [id])`, the only form `ledger.Verify` can recompute from the stored `{\"id\":…}` response.\n\nDecision for you: a replay with the same key and purchase but a different reason is treated as a replay and returns the original result, not 409. The reason is stored only in `ApprovalAudit`, and the database rules don't let requesters read that table, while `Verify` requires the hash to cover only the ID. Making it 409 would mean changing the schema or the ledger, which are outside the files I'm allowed to edit."
    ],
